And um the interesting thing was um that Dave um dug into the system a little bit and found that the policy network, you know, the neural network that um takes a position and gives a probability distribution over moves and basically allows you to pre-select promising moves, had assigned a very low probability of one in 10,000 to uh this particular move in that position.

36
00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:42.280
And so clearly the pattern matching system didn't think it was a good move.

37
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.480
And that makes a lot of sense because that system was trained on human data.

38
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:53.360
And so if it's surprising to a human, then it would be surprising to a system that's trained on human data.

39
00:03:53.360 --> 00:04:01.840
So it must have been the other part of the system, namely the game tree search that selected this move.

40
00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:08.880
And the game tree search, of course, looks at future variations and then judges the outcomes of that.

41
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:17.600
It's a reasoning process in the sense that I think is the right kind of reasoning that AI should be using.

42
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:31.680
And it was then that system that chose this move based on the consequences that unfold from that in its estimation in the game tree, and as judged by the value network.

43
00:04:31.680 --> 00:05:16.960
And so the general lesson, I think, that we can learn is that a system that is purely based on imitation, and that in our case, that was the policy network that was trained on human gameplay, but similarly, uh large language models that are trained to predict the next token on uh on human-generated text, that those are very unlikely to produce surprises or new, truly new knowledge, and that it is a dedicated reasoning process that looks at the consequences of an action or of a discovery that um that can uh break out of this convex hull of of human knowledge and truly discovery.

